.TH IMPORT 4
.SH NAME
import, 9export \- exchange name spaces with a Plan 9 system, unauthenticated
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import
.B -A
[
.B -ab
] [
.B -c
] [
.B -e clear
]
.I host
.I file
[
.I localfile
]
.PP
.B 9export
.B -A
[
.B -a
] [
.B -e clear
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
These speak Plan 9's
.I exportfs
protocol, with no authentication and in clear text:
anyone who can reach a
.I 9export
gets the name space it serves, and
.I import
takes the far end to be whoever answered.
They are for a network that is trusted.
Plan 9's own authentication
.RB ( p9sk1 )
is not in this system: its tickets are enciphered with DES,
which is no longer built into any kernel, hosted or native, and
.IR factotum (4)
refuses the protocol.
Each command therefore wants
.BR -A ,
Plan 9's own option for doing without, and without it says so and stops,
before anything is dialled or answered.
Between InferNode machines use
.IR mount (1)
and
.IR listen (1)
with
.IR export (4),
which authenticate with certificates and encrypt.
.PP
.I Import
dials
.I host
(which might be the current host) and makes the given
.I file
on that system visible in the local name space as
.I localfile
(if
.I localfile
is not given,
.I file
is used as the local name).
The far end must export in the same way:
.I 9export
.BR -A ,
or Plan 9's
.I exportfs
started without authentication.
The default service is
.BR exportfs .
The remote files are accessed with the permissions of whoever runs the
exporting command there.
.PP
If the remote
.I file
is a directory, the whole file tree rooted at that
directory is accessible, the local mount point must also be a directory,
and the
.BR -a ,
.BR -b ,
and
.B -c
options control the creation of union directories just as for
.IR bind (1).
By default, the remote file replaces the local one in the name space.
.PP
.I 9export
serves the protocol on its standard input,
allowing a Plan 9 system
(with its
.I import
.BR -A )
or another InferNode to import parts of the name space in which
.I 9export
is run.
A caller has 30 seconds to name its tree and say how it will
speak, and must ask for what is done here
.RB (` "impo nofilter clear" '):
one that asks for a filter or for encryption is refused, not served in
clear.
A tree that cannot be exported is refused without this system's reason for
it.
Normally
.I 9export
returns when the client closes the connection; the
.B -a
option causes
.I 9export
to return immediately, leaving a kernel process to serve the name space.
.I 9export
might be used as follows:
.IP
.EX
listen -Av 'tcp!*!exportfs' {9export -A&}
.EE
.PP
The
.B -e
option of either command, which once asked for the connection to be
encrypted, is refused unless it names
.BR clear :
with nobody authenticated there is no key to encrypt with.
The
.B -k
option, a key specification for
.IR factotum ,
is gone.
.PP
Beware that the name space is served with the permissions of the
person running
.IR 9export ,
not those of the caller.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/9export.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/import.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bind (1),
.IR listen (1),
.IR export (4),
.IR factotum (4)
